WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.330
- Hi, good evening, my
name is Alan Gerber.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:13.070
I'm the Director of the Institution

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:17.560
for Social and Policy Studies
and Dean of Social Sciences

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.960
at the Yale Faculty of Arts and Sciences.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:23.580
Welcome to tonight's events
on the 25th Amendment,

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.210
a constitutional mechanism

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.453
for removing the American President.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:31.900
This event is one of a series of webinars

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:33.823
that ISPS is offering.

00:00:35.209 --> 00:00:38.270
We've had a couple
already on the election.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:40.950
We're going to have some
more in coming months

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:45.950
as part of a program
called ISPS Democracy.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.580
ISPS Democracy is aimed
at supporting research

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:53.380
and discussion to further
understanding and improving

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:56.503
democratic representation
and accountability.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:00.910
These conversations and
the research that we do

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.023
is of course of great academic interests.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:08.280
But part of what brings us together today

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.060
is that we can see how
some of the work we do

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:18.060
collectively thinking and
writing about the problems

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.600
and issues in American democracy

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:28.970
after series of years turned
into the foundations for

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.970
and the tools we have for
navigating political crisis.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.810
So, this work that sort
of starts in the academy

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:44.347
can actually become an important
part of the real essence

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:51.913
of how we engage governance together.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:56.960
Tonight, we have a number
of distinguished scholars

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.840
of constitutional law
and American politics

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.997
to talk about the 25th
Amendment to the constitution.

00:02:04.997 --> 00:02:08.220
And this spring we'll have
a number of other events

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:12.050
as part of the ISPS Democracy program.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.203
And I just wanna tell you
about those very briefly.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:20.040
These are the tentatively
scheduled for February 9th,

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.730
we'll be having a webinar
on the new administration,

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:27.140
what's next for governance
and legislation?

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:30.670
On February the 23rd, a webinar

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:33.680
on rethinking political representation,

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.670
novel ideas for candidate recruitment,

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:41.030
voter education, deliberation
and policymaking.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:44.687
March the 16th, we'll be
having a webinar politics

00:02:44.687 --> 00:02:48.090
and a transformed
communications environment.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:50.260
And then later this spring,

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.040
I'll be working with a number of scholars

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.480
across the university on an idea

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.490
that we're still kind of putting together

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:03.920
but it's to think about policy making

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.060
and government performance during COVID-19

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:08.650
as a vehicle for exploring

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:11.360
both the specific policy failures,

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.320
but also general lessons about democracy

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.530
and policy making in democracy.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.930
So those are some of the
things we'll be doing

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:23.693
as part of ISPS Democracy
in the coming months.

