00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:16.130
First Akhil Amar, Professor Amar

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:17.750
is Sterling Professor of Law

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:20.680
and Political Science at Yale University.

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:22.910
And one of the nation's leading scholars

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:24.970
of constitutional law.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:27.780
His work has been cited
by Supreme Court justices

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:31.540
from across the spectrum
in more than 40 cases,

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:34.510
irregularly testifies before Congress,

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:37.930
is a member of the American
Academy of Arts and Sciences

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:40.460
and a recipient of the
American Bar Associations

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:42.520
outstanding scholar award.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:45.690
Is a graduate of Yale
College and Yale Law School.

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:49.000
And the author of dozens
of law review articles,

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.007
a number of books as well, including

00:05:52.007 --> 00:05:53.447
"The Constitution Today:

00:05:53.447 --> 00:05:57.210
"Timeless Lessons for
the Issues of Our Era."

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.460
He has written on the 25th Amendment,

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:04.100
creative ideas for how to
use the 25th Amendment.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:07.070
And he has an op-ed in today's I think,

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:08.200
New York Daily News

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:12.890
addressing the current political situation

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:15.590
in using the 25th Amendment

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:19.350
and how the 25th Amendment might be used

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:23.143
in response to the current
political challenges.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:27.350
Our second panelist is John Feerick.

00:06:28.881 --> 00:06:31.130
Professor Feerick is a
distinguished scholar

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:32.780
and academic leader.

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:34.940
The Norris professor of law

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:38.280
and former Dean of the Fordham
University of Law School

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:41.980
where he served as Dean
for about two decades.

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:45.250
He specializes in
constitutional law and theory,

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.393
employment, labor and arbitration.

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:52.690
In 2006 Fordham University established

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:56.800
the Feerick Center for Social
Justice at Fordham Law School.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:59.930
The center's mission is to
protect the underprivileged

00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:02.010
from a wide array of social ills

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:03.950
while working to bolster workers rights

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:06.670
and access to legal services.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:09.280
As editor in chief of
the Fordham Law Review

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:12.300
he wrote an article about
Presidential succession

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.790
which served as one of the foundations

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:19.380
for the 25th Amendment to the Constitution

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:22.970
which was an amendment
that he helped to craft

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:28.170
into the form that we see
in our constitution today.

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:31.810
So I will say I have
never had the privilege

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:36.280
I think of interacting with
somebody who helped to author

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:38.600
part of our constitution and so I just,

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:41.744
I couldn't be more pleased and honored

00:07:41.744 --> 00:07:45.713
that Professor Feerick is
here with us this evening.

00:07:46.915 --> 00:07:49.970
And then third, we have David Mayhew

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:53.740
a Sterling professor of
political science emeritus.

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:55.950
David is a fellow of the American Academy

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:59.940
of Arts and Sciences, the
American Philosophical Society,

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:02.540
and the National Academy of Sciences.

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:05.850
He is one of the nation's leading scholars

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:09.480
in American political history,
US legislative behavior,

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:12.220
political parties and policymaking.

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:14.960
He is a resident fellow of the institution

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:17.070
for social and policy studies

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:20.060
and the author of many landmark works,

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:23.610
including "Congress: The
Electoral Connection"

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:27.470
and "Divided We Govern" he
has an encyclopedic knowledge

00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:30.300
of American political history.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:34.230
And it is again a real
honor to have him with us

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:37.480
this evening to share his insights.

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:39.070
With that I just wanna turn it over

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:43.013
to Akhil and John and David,
thank you all very much.

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:46.110
- Thank you, Alan.

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:50.510
It's such an honor to be with
such an extraordinary panel.

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:54.420
I'm gonna try to actually be
a brief because I wanna hear

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:57.840
from the great John Feerick
and the great David Mayhew.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:01.050
I have to be especially
modest because John Feerick,

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:02.780
we're here to talk about
the 25th Amendment,

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:05.800
he not only basically
wrote the 25th Amendment,

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:08.210
he wrote the book on the 25th Amendment

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:10.237
called "The Twenty-Fifth Amendment."

00:09:11.978 --> 00:09:15.010
So, wow and as Alan said,

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:17.377
there's no one who knows
more about Congress

00:09:17.377 --> 00:09:20.610
who is living now and maybe has ever lived

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:22.710
than the great David Mayhew.

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:24.286
The 25th Amendment and it's such an honor

00:09:24.286 --> 00:09:26.310
to be with you both, really,

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:28.600
you guys are my role models in life.

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:32.660
And the 25th Amendment
has several provisions.

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:35.440
I think it was briefly
put up on the screen.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.970
Let me just mention a couple of them

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:43.940
and try to show you a larger structure.

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:46.380
Let me start with
something that John Feerick

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:50.920
I think wrote long ago that
before the 25th Amendment

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:55.920
we had no mechanism for filling
a Vice Presidential vacancy.

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:59.760
And for 40 out of the first 160 years,

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:03.080
the Vice Presidency was
basically empty at some point

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:05.930
either because the Vice
President had died or resigned

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:07.610
actually John C. Calhoun

00:10:07.610 --> 00:10:09.940
who's been at recently renamed proper,

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:14.190
resigned for a brief period or,

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:16.410
so 40 years in which
either the Vice President

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:19.780
was dead or resigned and
therefore there was a vacancy

00:10:19.780 --> 00:10:22.960
or the President had died

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:26.360
and the Vice President had moved up,

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:31.273
William Henry Harrison, Zachary
Taylor, Abraham Lincoln,

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:37.160
James Garfield, McKinley
and so on, Warren Harding.

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:40.410
So the President had died
and the Vice President

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:43.610
moved up to fill the Presidency
and there wasn't a mechanism

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:45.510
for filling that Vice
Presidential vacancy.

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:48.530
For 40 years there's this emptiness.

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:50.940
Section one of the 25th Amendment says,

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:52.083
here's how we fill it.

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:57.590
The President basically nominate
someone to fill that spot.

00:10:57.590 --> 00:11:00.060
And then there's a congressional
confirmation process

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:01.630
that's a little bit more elaborate,

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:03.960
then the ordinary cabinet
one involves the House

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:05.250
as well as the Senate.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:09.900
That's how, when Spiro
Agnew, and then all of this,

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:13.770
let me take a step back
is after the assassination

00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:17.430
of President Kennedy, when
Lyndon Johnson becomes President

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:19.490
and the Vice Presidency remains vacant,

00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:22.238
the one problem do is
you really wanna have

00:11:22.238 --> 00:11:25.640
a Vice Presidential vacancy which requires

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:29.600
then if anything happens,
a statutory succession

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:31.480
and the statutes currently

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:35.480
provide for the Speaker of
the House as next in line.

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:36.780
And I'm not sure if that makes sense

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:38.800
and I'm not sure that makes
sense, given the 25th Amendment.

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:39.830
So I'm gonna actually, again

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:42.440
try to show you some of
the implications of this.

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:47.440
So Kennedy is killed,
Johnson becomes President.

00:11:49.096 --> 00:11:51.493
There's no way of filling that
Vice Presidential vacancy.

00:11:51.493 --> 00:11:54.150
John Feerick writes a piece about that.

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:55.370
The Senate gets involved,

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:57.173
the House they adopt this Amendment.

00:11:58.240 --> 00:11:59.480
And the first thing that they say is,

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:01.170
given there's a Vice Presidential vacancy

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:04.080
the President can fill the
slot, that section one.

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:06.310
And so when Spiro Agnew resigns

00:12:06.310 --> 00:12:07.783
'cause he was at crook,

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:14.900
Nixon picks Ford under the
25th Amendment Section 1

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:17.450
and Ford's confirmed eventually.

00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:19.930
And then when Nixon resigns, Ford moves up

00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:23.530
and under the 25th Amendment,
he picks Nelson Rockefeller.

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:24.660
Now here's one thing

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:26.960
if you think deeply
about the logic of that

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:28.360
you vote for Republicans,

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:30.320
you should get Republicans for four years.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:34.020
You get Nixon if not Nixon,
Agnew, if not Agnew, Ford,

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:37.630
if not Ford, Rockefeller
kind of Apostolic succession

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:40.900
but boy that's the logic of section one,

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:42.950
the current Succession Act is weird

00:12:42.950 --> 00:12:47.950
because you vote for Trump and Trump Pence

00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:50.210
and you could end up with Pelosi.

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:55.210
You vote for Obama and you
can end up with Paul Ryan.

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:59.843
You vote for Reagan and you
end up with Tip O'Neill.

00:13:01.270 --> 00:13:04.130
Now my friends that's
deeply destabilizing.

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:07.480
In fact, put it mildly, it's
an assassination incentive.

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:10.177
If you can simultaneously
knock out President

00:13:10.177 --> 00:13:13.380
and Vice President, you could
accomplish regime change.

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:16.970
If you do it seriatim, oh
you knock out one person

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:19.070
someone else moves up and
then they pick a successor

00:13:19.070 --> 00:13:20.970
and it's apostolic succession.

00:13:20.970 --> 00:13:23.790
That is somehow both of them went at once,

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:25.444
imagine a bomb or something like that.

00:13:25.444 --> 00:13:28.380
Now party control lurches to the folks

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:30.490
who lost that presidential election

00:13:30.490 --> 00:13:32.440
is that the right approach?

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:34.810
The 25th Amendment says the Vice President

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:37.560
should be basically occupied by someone

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:40.430
who works hand in glove
with the President.

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:43.670
The President hand-picked,
beginning in the 20th century

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:45.910
really Presidents handpicked
their running mates,

00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:47.460
that wasn't true before,

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:49.070
but by the middle of the 20th century,

00:13:49.070 --> 00:13:51.970
that's basically true.

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:54.420
Ike through the nomination,

00:13:54.420 --> 00:13:57.210
open to the convention
in '56 with the Kefauve

00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:00.210
but usually Presidents
pick their number twos,

00:14:00.210 --> 00:14:03.740
Batman and Robin they can
work closely together,

00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:05.290
but that's not true for you see

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:07.097
the Speaker of the House necessarily.

00:14:07.097 --> 00:14:11.020
So, section one has implications
for statutory succession.

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:14.040
There are other provisions
of the amendment,

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:18.440
one it says, when the
present just voluntarily

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:20.480
needs to step aside for a moment

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:23.380
he's gonna undergo a
colonoscopy or something else

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:25.330
that's gonna put him out for a while,

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:30.330
he can voluntarily hand over
power to the Vice President.

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:32.010
And that's what I wrote about today.

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:33.810
And maybe Trump can do that today.

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:36.950
He can, he doesn't have to
resign he can just step aside

00:14:36.950 --> 00:14:39.060
and put Pence in charge

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:42.170
as if you were undergoing a colonoscopy

00:14:42.170 --> 00:14:45.170
for eight days, for 10
days, he needs a rest.

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:48.500
So, but that works best
when there's a teamwork

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:50.661
between President and Vice President,

00:14:50.661 --> 00:14:54.540
they basically are mates,
they're running mates

00:14:55.610 --> 00:14:58.370
but that doesn't work again
with the Speaker of the House

00:14:58.370 --> 00:15:00.290
who might be someone
different political party,

00:15:00.290 --> 00:15:03.580
in section four, which
you've heard a lot about is

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:06.230
an involuntary transfer of power,

00:15:06.230 --> 00:15:09.070
not triggered by the President,

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:11.760
but when the Vice President

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:14.810
thinks that the President
is disabled in some way,

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:15.930
mentally or physically.

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:19.230
Remember this is triggered
by Kennedy's assassination,

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:22.120
the Vice President with
the approval of the cabinet

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:25.080
or some other body can
displace the President

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:27.050
but what if the President challenges that,

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:29.150
there are some provisions of all of that.

00:15:29.150 --> 00:15:30.780
So, the 25th Amendment

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:33.367
has several different provisions in it

00:15:33.367 --> 00:15:35.940
and we need to understand
how it fits together

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:38.380
it has all sorts of
interesting implications

00:15:38.380 --> 00:15:43.380
beyond just the one right now
could Pence displace Trump?

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:45.620
Could Trump step aside?

00:15:45.620 --> 00:15:48.010
And other implications
too, I said enough for now.

00:15:48.010 --> 00:15:49.930
I wanna get of course, John and David in

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:52.140
and then I'm sure we'll
have a lot of back and forth

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:55.050
but if you have any real questions about

00:15:55.988 --> 00:15:58.070
the amendment that we don't cover today

00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:01.100
this is the Bible that's
John Feerick's book.

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:02.520
And you're about to hear from John Feerick

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:04.020
'cause he wrote the amendment.

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:07.930
- I wanna thank you very much Akhil.

00:16:07.930 --> 00:16:10.140
I will just add in terms of the book

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:14.680
that its value is reflected
in the Barnes &amp; Noble

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:16.563
and other bookstores is $10.

00:16:17.490 --> 00:16:21.760
Had because it was
reprinted by Barnes &amp; Nobles

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:24.140
to make it more available.

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:28.940
But to the subject,
I'd like to start with,

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:30.100
one I wanna say it's an honor

00:16:30.100 --> 00:16:31.920
to be part of this program tonight

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:34.443
with you Akhil and Professor Mayhew

00:16:34.443 --> 00:16:39.220
and to be at Yale Law School as well.

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:40.543
A great, great law school.

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:45.040
I start with the original
succession provision

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:49.490
which is in Article 2 of the Constitution.

00:16:49.490 --> 00:16:54.490
And my sentences of it is as follows

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:57.620
that it deals with a contingency of death,

00:16:57.620 --> 00:17:02.110
resignation, removal and
inability of a President

00:17:02.110 --> 00:17:03.523
and also a Vice President,

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:09.890
it's ambiguous as to what
happens in a case of death,

00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:14.430
resignation, removal, and the ability.

00:17:14.430 --> 00:17:17.700
It just says the same shall
devolve on the Vice President.

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:20.650
So it leaves open the
question of the status

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:22.963
in a succession contingency.

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:27.420
There's no definition of inability,

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:30.690
there's nothing in the
original constitution

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:33.681
as to how you go about judging

00:17:33.681 --> 00:17:36.850
the existence of a presidential inability.

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:41.320
And the second major
provision of Article 2

00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:43.970
of the Constitution is it
gives Congress the power

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:46.340
to establish a line of succession.

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:49.440
And we've had three lines
of succession in our history

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:54.200
and initially we had the
two leaders of Congress

00:17:54.200 --> 00:17:56.057
in the line of succession
starting with the President

00:17:56.057 --> 00:17:58.070
from 10 then the Speaker,

00:17:58.070 --> 00:18:00.440
in 1886 the line of succession

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:02.800
went to the cabinet departments.

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:06.430
And in 1947, the line of succession

00:18:06.430 --> 00:18:08.840
started with legislative
leaders in a reverse order,

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:11.110
Speaker and then the president pro tem

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:13.300
in the light of a cabinet line.

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:15.890
And that's the existing
line of succession.

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:20.890
Now, when the first President
died, William Harrison

00:18:22.390 --> 00:18:24.150
an issue arose as to the status

00:18:24.150 --> 00:18:26.610
of the Vice President John Tyler.

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:29.300
And he claimed that under
the succession provision

00:18:29.300 --> 00:18:32.050
when you read the succession
provision, he said,

00:18:32.050 --> 00:18:36.060
in a case of death, President had died,

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:38.440
I become the President, I
get the title of President

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:40.160
and I serve for the rest of the term.

00:18:40.160 --> 00:18:43.133
Some members of Congress at
the time said, no, no, no.

00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:46.407
And John Quincy Adams
said that in his diary,

00:18:46.407 --> 00:18:48.627
"No, no, no, you're an acting President."

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:54.550
And, you haven't, but Tyler made it stick.

00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:57.600
And that became the
precedent in the future

00:18:57.600 --> 00:18:59.200
when Presidents died in office

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:00.470
they followed the Tyler President,

00:19:00.470 --> 00:19:02.910
I become the President
for the rest of the term

00:19:02.910 --> 00:19:04.800
of existing President.

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:08.300
However, that created a further ambiguity

00:19:08.300 --> 00:19:13.300
because and in 1881, when
President Garfield was shot

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:20.253
and he would eventually die
80 days after being shot,

00:19:22.218 --> 00:19:26.350
it was not clear how to deal
with a case of inability,

00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:31.100
because inability was in
the same clause as a death.

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:36.100
And some said, well, if the
Vice President takes over

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:39.800
the powers and duties,
he becomes the President

00:19:39.800 --> 00:19:41.570
regardless of whether
the President recovers

00:19:41.570 --> 00:19:45.650
from the apparent inability at that point,

00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:47.810
in terms of those 80 days.

00:19:47.810 --> 00:19:50.580
And so there was, and
then it was not clear

00:19:51.470 --> 00:19:54.650
who could make a
determination of eligibility.

00:19:54.650 --> 00:19:58.420
Chester Arthur the Vice
President he wasn't interested

00:19:58.420 --> 00:20:00.740
in getting involved in
making the determination.

00:20:00.740 --> 00:20:02.540
He came from a different wing of the party

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:06.945
and even the assassin implicated

00:20:06.945 --> 00:20:10.228
that wing of the party, so to speak.

00:20:10.228 --> 00:20:13.950
So that was, it became a dead provision.

00:20:13.950 --> 00:20:16.380
It was not clear who made the judgment

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:18.640
and also what the status was.

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:21.683
And now we move into jump
ahead to the Eisenhower years.

00:20:22.549 --> 00:20:26.150
He had three, at least three
presidential inabilities.

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:28.200
And a heart attack and stroke,

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:33.200
he had an internal
issue and he wasn't able

00:20:35.590 --> 00:20:40.220
for a week or more than a
week in that time period.

00:20:40.220 --> 00:20:43.080
And he was anxious to his Attorney General

00:20:43.080 --> 00:20:45.320
a graduate of Yale Law
School, Herbert Brownell

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:48.350
former editor in chief
of the Yale Law Review.

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:51.570
We got to deal with this constitutionally

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:55.560
and they launched the hearings at the time

00:20:55.560 --> 00:20:57.680
and the house was very active

00:20:57.680 --> 00:20:59.860
through it's a chair of
the Judiciary Committee,

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:01.513
Emanuel Celler of New York.

00:21:02.681 --> 00:21:05.610
But they couldn't really come to agreement

00:21:05.610 --> 00:21:07.850
on how to deal with this subject

00:21:07.850 --> 00:21:09.310
where President's disabled

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:14.230
and we'll figure it out
when it happens so to speak.

00:21:14.230 --> 00:21:16.010
It was the result of all that.

00:21:16.010 --> 00:21:20.637
Well, Eisenhower used to command, right?

00:21:20.637 --> 00:21:22.970
And in terms of his military history

00:21:22.970 --> 00:21:25.480
and outstanding military
history, and he said,

00:21:25.480 --> 00:21:26.900
well I'm gonna work out a protocol

00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:29.560
with my Vice President, Richard Nixon.

00:21:29.560 --> 00:21:31.610
And I'm sure Herbert Brownell

00:21:31.610 --> 00:21:36.380
and his successor were very
much involved in those ideas.

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:38.400
And that is to say we're gonna develop

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:40.860
what we think the constitution means

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:43.100
and what we think the constitution means.

00:21:43.100 --> 00:21:44.820
And we're gonna put it in a letter,

00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:48.200
a letter between the President
and the Vice President,

00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:53.200
and that letter and I think
roughly March of 1958 said that

00:21:58.370 --> 00:22:00.520
and this was Eisenhower's idea

00:22:02.683 --> 00:22:05.640
that if I wanna declare my disability

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:09.100
because I want to have an
operation as Professor Amar said,

00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:10.790
for a colonoscopy the next day

00:22:10.790 --> 00:22:13.480
and I'm gonna be unconscious
during part of the operation.

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:17.610
I'm gonna pass over my
powers and duties to you

00:22:18.610 --> 00:22:22.150
Vice President Nixon, you take
over as the acting President

00:22:22.150 --> 00:22:24.460
and I'll tell you when I'm
ready to come back again.

00:22:24.460 --> 00:22:26.380
So that's sort of the first protocol

00:22:26.380 --> 00:22:28.160
they worked out in the sleeve.

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:30.053
And then the second protocol had to do

00:22:30.053 --> 00:22:33.580
with a situation where
if I, says Eisenhower

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:37.100
if I'm unable to determine my inability

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:39.030
I want you to have the power to,

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:41.110
you have the power to say that

00:22:41.110 --> 00:22:45.260
and have some consultation
before you exercise the power.

00:22:45.260 --> 00:22:49.640
And that's the second
strand of that letter.

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:51.433
And the third strand was,

00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:54.760
in any event whether it's the first strand

00:22:54.760 --> 00:22:56.470
or the second strand, as soon as I said

00:22:56.470 --> 00:22:59.670
I'm ready to take it over, I get it back.

00:22:59.670 --> 00:23:03.030
And that letter agreement then
flows into the Kennedy years

00:23:03.030 --> 00:23:05.930
between President Kennedy
and Lyndon Johnson.

00:23:05.930 --> 00:23:08.338
And then when President
Kennedy is assassinated

00:23:08.338 --> 00:23:11.870
and Lyndon Johnson becomes the President

00:23:11.870 --> 00:23:13.500
he works out the same agreement

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:15.270
with the Speaker of the
House of Representatives

00:23:15.270 --> 00:23:17.260
because he's the next
in line of succession,

00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:19.730
we don't have a Vice President
but we have no procedure

00:23:19.730 --> 00:23:22.230
for filling a vacancy
in the Vice Presidency.

00:23:22.230 --> 00:23:24.570
And at the time that that happened,

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:26.890
so what happens if the
President was disabled,

00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:29.410
what you would see in the
newspapers at the time

00:23:29.410 --> 00:23:31.370
and suppose Kennedy didn't die

00:23:31.370 --> 00:23:34.052
and he lingered between life and death

00:23:34.052 --> 00:23:37.993
as Garfield did, how
would we deal with that?

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:41.520
We would have had that the protocols

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:44.220
of the Eisenhower administration
but some would say,

00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:46.900
well, that's not law,
that's just an understanding

00:23:46.900 --> 00:23:48.560
between the President
and the Vice President,

00:23:48.560 --> 00:23:51.010
we're talking about the constitution here.

00:23:51.010 --> 00:23:53.970
So at that point, Senator
Birch Bayh of Indiana

00:23:55.260 --> 00:23:58.020
inherits all the ideas that were around

00:23:58.020 --> 00:24:00.910
during the Eisenhower
years and inherits the work

00:24:00.910 --> 00:24:03.270
of the Senate Judiciary Committee

00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:05.100
and the House Judiciary Committee

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:08.000
with the different proposals
and different ideas.

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:11.040
And he comes up with a framework

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:16.040
that's not too far away
from the letter agreement.

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:20.470
And as Professor Mayhew has said,

00:24:20.470 --> 00:24:23.070
the framework is President
can declare his own disability

00:24:23.070 --> 00:24:25.220
under the 25th Amendment.

00:24:25.220 --> 00:24:27.140
And as soon as he's ready to come back,

00:24:27.140 --> 00:24:29.360
he comes back and it can't be any check

00:24:29.360 --> 00:24:31.513
on the section three
about him coming back.

00:24:32.500 --> 00:24:34.730
And in terms of section four,

00:24:34.730 --> 00:24:39.730
which has a lot of detail
in it, section four says,

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:43.800
well, the Vice President
and not to consult, but,

00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:47.140
and a majority of the cabinet,

00:24:47.140 --> 00:24:48.910
heads of the executive departments

00:24:48.910 --> 00:24:51.690
commonly referred to as the cabinet,

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:53.540
but there might be others
who sit in a cabinet

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:54.890
who are not in that group.

00:24:54.890 --> 00:24:58.260
Roughly, I think there are
15 department heads today.

00:24:58.260 --> 00:25:02.980
So you need votes of
eight department heads

00:25:02.980 --> 00:25:05.310
and sometimes it might be
an acting department head

00:25:05.310 --> 00:25:07.820
because department head has left

00:25:07.820 --> 00:25:10.190
and somebody has had to step up,

00:25:10.190 --> 00:25:13.480
some people will create an issue

00:25:13.480 --> 00:25:15.270
as to whether that person can vote.

00:25:15.270 --> 00:25:20.270
I think they can and then
suppose the President

00:25:21.490 --> 00:25:23.930
doesn't accept that
declaration of inability,

00:25:23.930 --> 00:25:25.520
what happens then?

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:30.520
Well, once he says, I'm
able, I don't agree with you

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:35.540
Vice President and cabinet, I
think I'm ready to, I'm okay.

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:37.413
I'm not what you think I am.

00:25:41.135 --> 00:25:45.734
And they then have to evaluate
what the President said,

00:25:45.734 --> 00:25:48.600
and they got four days to do
so under the 25th Amendment.

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:51.100
And if they disagree with the President,

00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:53.010
first it's gonna go over to Congress.

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:55.710
But when they make the
original determination

00:25:55.710 --> 00:25:57.350
the Vice President immediately takes over

00:25:57.350 --> 00:25:59.320
as the Acting President.

00:25:59.320 --> 00:26:02.830
And all the powers to his
that of the Presidency.

00:26:02.830 --> 00:26:04.980
President doesn't lose the Presidency,

00:26:04.980 --> 00:26:06.480
he's just taken over the discharge

00:26:06.480 --> 00:26:08.750
of the powers of a Presidency,

00:26:08.750 --> 00:26:12.950
and that's what, and
he's gonna hold onto that

00:26:12.950 --> 00:26:16.160
the Vice President because it's
been a question of capacity

00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:18.950
that's been raised and
rather than have it back out,

00:26:18.950 --> 00:26:22.210
go back and forth, he's
gonna stay in the process

00:26:22.210 --> 00:26:25.150
of the Acting President
until it plays itself out.

00:26:25.150 --> 00:26:28.290
And if there is a disagreement,
it goes to Congress

00:26:28.290 --> 00:26:30.400
and it takes a two thirds vote

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:32.573
of each House of Congress to say,

00:26:33.420 --> 00:26:34.870
whatever it's gonna say

00:26:34.870 --> 00:26:37.550
which effectively means
that the President is secure

00:26:37.550 --> 00:26:41.250
as the vote of one third
plus one of either House

00:26:41.250 --> 00:26:43.870
at that point, he resumes
his powers and duties

00:26:43.870 --> 00:26:47.700
and the House has, the Senate has 21 days

00:26:47.700 --> 00:26:50.840
or both Houses have 21
days to take the process.

00:26:50.840 --> 00:26:53.150
And if they do nothing
at the end of 21 days

00:26:53.150 --> 00:26:55.520
the President resumes
his powers and duties.

00:26:55.520 --> 00:26:59.500
But as I say, as soon as
one House plus one say

00:26:59.500 --> 00:27:00.520
we agree with the President

00:27:00.520 --> 00:27:03.010
he immediately resumes
his powers and duties.

00:27:03.010 --> 00:27:06.660
So, and, but keep in mind
under the 25th Amendment

00:27:07.510 --> 00:27:09.593
after eight days from now,

00:27:11.320 --> 00:27:16.110
it's moot because the
President's term is over.

00:27:16.110 --> 00:27:21.110
And the Acting President,
current Vice President

00:27:21.710 --> 00:27:23.210
no longer has an office.

00:27:23.210 --> 00:27:26.330
And it doesn't pass to his successor

00:27:26.330 --> 00:27:31.330
because the President
that he was acting for

00:27:32.310 --> 00:27:34.290
has left office.

00:27:34.290 --> 00:27:37.090
So, eight days from now, whatever it is

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:40.563
the 25th Amendment is moot.

00:27:42.162 --> 00:27:46.620
The one thing that it does is
during those eight wet days

00:27:46.620 --> 00:27:50.200
the powers and duties are
with the Vice President

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:52.793
so that if there are gonna be any pardons,

00:27:54.419 --> 00:27:56.170
the President doesn't
have the pardoning power

00:27:56.170 --> 00:27:58.127
all of that is with the Vice President,

00:27:58.127 --> 00:28:02.890
'cause he's taken over all
the powers of the Presidency.

00:28:02.890 --> 00:28:07.180
And if there's, if the
Vice President and cabinet

00:28:07.180 --> 00:28:10.370
believe there's a risk to
the security of the nation

00:28:10.370 --> 00:28:12.213
because of threats in the world,

00:28:14.050 --> 00:28:16.330
the person who has control
of it during that period

00:28:16.330 --> 00:28:17.520
is the Acting President

00:28:18.500 --> 00:28:23.500
so that sort of gets you
into the 25th Amendment.

00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:27.800
And, it went through Congress
with support by Democrats

00:28:27.800 --> 00:28:30.950
and Republicans in both Houses of Congress

00:28:30.950 --> 00:28:33.070
and they worked together

00:28:33.070 --> 00:28:35.223
and all the detail that you see on there.

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:41.820
And it was, the ABA
played a very major role

00:28:42.120 --> 00:28:44.077
in the knowledge and
the legislative history.

00:28:44.077 --> 00:28:47.220
And my involvement was through
the American Bar Association,

00:28:47.220 --> 00:28:48.970
asked me to be on a committee of 12

00:28:50.040 --> 00:28:53.530
that was given this
opportunity so to speak,

00:28:53.530 --> 00:28:55.530
to make recommendations,

00:28:55.530 --> 00:28:59.537
we worked very closely
with the Senator Birch Bayh

00:28:59.537 --> 00:29:00.950
and those in Congress

00:29:01.900 --> 00:29:05.410
and it was in the constitution
in February of 1967.

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:07.340
I may have run out of my seven minutes

00:29:07.340 --> 00:29:11.420
but let me just end by saying that

